                G N A T S T A C K  E X A M P L E S
                ==================================

This directory contains various examples that demonstrate how to use GNATstack.
Each subdirectory contains a small example illustrating a particular point of
the technology.

In this README file, the '$' sign denotes the system prompt.

Important notice: if you are on a multiuser machine and get the present example
directory from a shared gnat installation, you may not be able to compile
directly in this directory.
In this situation, you will need to copy this directory
to another directory where you have the write permission. For instance,
on many Unix sustems, you will do:

  $ cp -r <prefix>/examples <another directory>

You can compile and run all the examples by simply running:

  $ make

If you are on a  Windows system and use the Cygwin environment, in order to
use  the Makefiles from the cygwin shell you need to type:

  $ make CYGWIN=true

If you are using a cross compiler you need to type:

  $ make TARGET=<target name>

where target is the prefix of your GNAT commands. For instance, if you are
using a cross compiler for PowerPC with VxWorks you need to type:

  $ make TARGET=powerpc-wrs-vxworks

If you want to change the default location of the run-time library you need to
type:

  $ make RTS=<rts>

where rts is eather a path (relative or absolute) to the run-time library or
the name of a GNAT predefined run time (such as zfp for the Zero FootPrint run
time).

You can also compile and run the examples located in a given directory by
typing:

  $ make <directory>

in this directory, or by doing:

  $ cd <directory>
  $ make

You can also type

  $ make clean

anywhere to remove the files produced at compilation time.

Here's a list of the various subdirectories:

simple:

    This directory contains a very simple example that shows how to compile and
    analyze the stack requirements of an application.

simple_project:

    This directory is an introduction to the use of project files with
    GNATstack.

full_project:

    This directory is a follow-up to the previous one. It demonstrates how to
    use some advanced features of project files, such as project hierarchies
    and mixed-language applications.

problem_detection:

    This directory shows some useful GNATstack options to perform additional
    analysis, such as detection of indirect calls, recursion, missing stack
    information, and dynamic frames.

           Thank you for using GNATstack, and happy training!
